msgstr "Converged"

#: ../../electronic/structure_optimization.rst:256
msgid ""
"初回は値が同じなので、違いは直線探索の精度から生じたものではない。 "
"この比較ではBFGSが1回目に得た曲率の情報を使うことで、2回目の更新から差が出る。"
msgstr ""
"The values agree on the first step, so the difference does not come from "
"the accuracy of the line search. BFGS uses curvature information from the"
" first step, and the results diverge from the second step onward."

#: ../../electronic/structure_optimization.rst:262
msgid "解釈と限界"
msgstr "Interpretation and Limitations"

#: ../../electronic/structure_optimization.rst:264
msgid ""
"**収束の対象。** 固定セルの力の収束は、そのセルで原子配置が停留点に 達したことを示す。可変セルで同じ判断をするには、許されたセル変形に "
"対する残留応力も判定する。対称性で拘束した方向の安定性には別の評価が要る。"
msgstr ""
"**What converges.** Force convergence in a fixed cell shows that the "
"atomic configuration has reached a stationary point for that cell. To "
"make the corresponding judgment for a variable cell, also check the "
"residual stress in the allowed deformation directions. Stability in "
"directions constrained by symmetry requires a separate assessment."

#: ../../electronic/structure_optimization.rst:267
msgid ""
"**反復回数。** 上の比較は、二次形式で直線上の極小を毎回正確に 求めた場合の値である。実材料では曲率が途中で変わり、エネルギーや "
"勾配にも数値誤差がある。反復回数だけで手法を比べず、最後の力や応力と 目的の構造量が一致するかを比べる。"
msgstr ""
"**Iteration count.** The comparison above uses a quadratic form and finds"
" the exact minimum along the search line at every step. In real "
"materials, the curvature changes during optimization, and the energies "
"and gradients have numerical errors. When comparing methods, look beyond "
"the iteration count and check whether the final forces or stresses and "
"the structural quantities of interest agree."

#: ../../electronic/structure_optimization.rst:271
msgid ""
"**局所構造と相安定性。** 異なる初期構造が異なる極小に落ちる材料では、 一つの最適化で見つけた構造だけから全体の最安定相は決まらない。 "
"複数の相を比較するときは、拘束、外圧、エネルギーの基準をそろえる。 有限温度の相安定性を求めるには、熱による自由エネルギーの寄与も要る。"
msgstr ""
"**Local structure and phase stability.** If different starting structures"
" lead to different minima, the structure found by a single optimization "
"does not identify the most stable phase overall. Compare phases using the"
" same constraints, external pressure, and energy reference. Assessing "
"phase stability at finite temperature also requires thermal contributions"
" to the free energy."

